  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Clearing skies and cooler temperatures expected\nthrough Sunday morning.\n\nA few lingering showers and low clouds this morning through \nsunrise as the cold front finally pushes offshore. Already \nseeing clear skies across northern CT and western MA, and \nexpecting those to overspread the rest of southern New England \n5-7AM.\n\nBehind the cold front, a rather strong continental high \npressure system builds over the Great Lakes and southern \nOntario. Northwest winds will advect those cooler temperatures \nand lower dewpoints into New England, sharply dropping surface \ntemperatures through the weekend. Today's highs are likely to be\non the order of 10-15 degrees less than yesterday's, with \novernight temps dropping into the low 40s (low 50s on the Cape \nand Islands).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for a few scattered showers Sunday \nnight, generally south of the Mass Pike. A secondary, sharp \ntemperature drop is then expected for the start of next week \nfollowed by a return to dry, fall-like conditions.\n\nThe last few model runs have remained consistent in indicating \na front lifting north off the DelVarMa coast, bringing potential\nfor more scattered showers to southern New England late Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. Some uncertainty remains in just how \nfar north these showers might reach, but highest potential at \nthis time remains south of the Mass Pike.\n\nA strong high pressure system is then expected to build in \nright on the heels of a deep Canadian low, bringing a secondary \nshot of even colder air to New England for the start of next \nwork week. Latest guidance indicates the 540mb line sinking well\ninto New England, possibly as far south as central MA by early \nTuesday morning. 850mb temperatures likely drop to near or just \nbelow 0C by Tuesday morning. This would support surface highs \nnear 60 degrees with nighttime lows possibly falling into the \n30s through Wednesday.\n\nThe high then pushes east by Thursday, marking the return of \ndrier conditions, southwest flow, and seasonable temperature to \nsouthern New England for the end of the week.",
